course: dictionary
lesson: 15907
title: Unmerited: not deserved
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unmerited means not deserved. The start, un-, means not, and the rest is about earning something. «The punishment was unmerited: Ava had done nothing wrong.» Ava did not deserve the punishment, so it was unfair.
explain: It can be good or bad. «The judge's praise was unmerited, since Ben had not even tried.» Here the praise was not earned, so it is praise that does not match what Ben did. Unmerited can describe a gift too: «She received unmerited favour, a prize she had done nothing to earn.»
explain: This is a formal word, so you will often find it in books, speeches and news. To check your meaning, swap in "not deserved" or "not earned". If the sentence still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: Unmerited means not deserved, whether good or bad.
fact: The start un- means not, so unmerited means not earned or deserved.
fact: «Unmerited punishment» is unfair, because the person did nothing to deserve it.
fact: «Unmerited praise» is praise that has not been earned.
fact: In church talk, Christians speak of God's grace as unmerited favour: a gift that is not earned.
exercise: «The punishment was unmerited, because Ava had done nothing wrong.» What does unmerited mean here? | Sung loudly | Earned after hard work | *Not deserved | Very large
exercise: Ben's teacher gave him unmerited praise for a story he had not written. What does unmerited praise mean? | Praise written in a book | *Praise that is not earned | Praise shouted from a hill | Praise that is well earned
exercise: Jayden says unmerited is just a fancy word for lucky. Is he right? | *No, it means not deserved, which is not the same as luck | Yes, it means happy | Yes, it means lucky | No, it means tired
test: «The judge's praise was unmerited, since Ben had not even tried.» Unmerited means the praise was | Written down | Sung loudly | Well earned | *Not earned or deserved
test: Which word is the closest meaning to unmerited? | *Undeserved | Earned | Quick | Hidden
exam: Which sentence uses unmerited correctly? | *The prize was unmerited, because Sam had not practised at all. | The prize was unmerited, because Sam had practised every day for a year. | The prize was unmerited, so Sam was very tired. | The prize was unmerited, because Sam was given a cake.
